24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nTwelve 1 by 1 squares form a rectangle, as shown. What is the total area of the shaded region? [asy]\nsize(4cm);defaultpen(linewidth(0.75));\n\n// Filled portions\nfill((0, 4)--(0, 0)--(2, 0)--cycle, gray(0.75));\nfill((0, 4)--(3, 4)--(3, 0)--cycle, gray(0.75));\n\n// grid\nint j;\nfor (j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {draw((j, 0)--(j, 4));}\nfor (j = 0; j < 5; ++j) {draw((0, j)--(3, j));}\n\n//diagonals\ndraw((0, 4)--(3, 0)); draw((0, 4)--(2, 0));\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cdf61772b42c13df23e43df88ecdab68c0450fcdf0f99335f849d465ebb99083"},"seconds":11.568883657455444,"seed":744437086184201778,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the total area of the shaded region, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the number of 1 by 1 squares in the rectangle:**\n   The rectangle is composed of 12 unit squares.\n\n2. **Analyze the shaded regions:**\n   - The first shaded region is a right triangle with vertices at \\((0, 4)\\), \\((0, 0)\\), and \\((2, 0)\\).\n   - The second shaded region is a larger right triangle with vertices at \\((0, 4)\\), \\((3, 4)\\), and \\((3, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each shaded triangle:**\n   - For the first triangle:\n     - Base: 2 units (from \\(x=0\\) to \\(x=2\\))\n     - Height: 4 units (from \\(y=4\\) to \\(y=0\\))\n     - Area of a triangle is given by \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\\)\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area}_1 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4 = 4 \\text{ square units}\n     \\]\n\n   - For the second triangle:\n     - Base: 3 units (from \\(x=0\\) to \\(x=3\\))\n     - Height: 4 units (from \\(y=4\\) to \\(y=0\\))\n     - Area of a triangle is given by \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\\)\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area}_2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 4 = 6 \\text{ square units}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Sum the areas of the two shaded triangles:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total shaded area} = \\text{Area}_1 + \\text{Area}_2 = 4 + 6 = 10 \\text{ square units}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total area of the shaded region is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{10}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:696"}
